# gitleaks findings allowed BY FINGERPRINT (`commit:file:rule:startline`).
#
# Read `.gitleaks.toml` first: allowlisting a fake fixture BY VALUE there is this repo's normal
# route, and it covers past and future commits at once. This file exists for the case that route
# cannot reach — a literal that is GONE from the source but still sits in committed diffs.
#
# WHY A FINGERPRINT FILE AT ALL. `gitleaks detect` scans git HISTORY (816 commits at the time of
# writing), so deleting a value from a working-tree file does not clear a finding whose diff is
# already committed. The only other way to clear it is rewriting history, which the operator
# considered and REJECTED on 2026-08-13. These three lines are the other half of that decision.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-13 — three findings, ONE synthetic value, `generic-api-key` rule.
#
# All three are the same string used as a `TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY` test fixture in the three GSC
# `.db.test.ts` files named below. It is not a credential and never was one: it is a hand-written
# counting pattern — 1a 2b 3c 4d 5e 6f 70 81 92 a3 b4 c5 d6 e7 f8 09 — written out twice to reach
# 32 bytes, and each commit that introduced it carries the comment "Unmistakably a test value,
# never a real key". Nothing was rotated because there is nothing to rotate: no live system, env
# var or deploy secret has ever held this value. It seals fixture rows in a local Supabase during
# `test:db` and is thrown away with them.
#
# The source no longer contains the literal either (removed in the same commit that added this
# file): all three files now DERIVE the key with `createHash("sha256")` over a fixed public
# phrase. So no future commit can reintroduce it and no fourth fingerprint can appear here —
# these three cover exactly the three commits already in history.
